  "answer": "**No. The Aussie dollar is strong against the yen right now, though it has eased a little from its recent peak.**\n\n**Latest rate:** A$1 = **¥109.67** (RBA, 4pm rate on 16 October 2026)\n\n**Compared with earlier periods:**\n- **A year ago:** ¥100.84 at the end of October 2025. The dollar now buys about **9% more yen**.\n- **18 months ago:** ¥91.43 at the end of April 2025, the low point of the past two years. It's now about **20% higher**.\n- **Since 2023:** month-end rates have ranged from about ¥89 to ¥114.5, and today's rate is near the top of that range.\n\n**The recent dip:** the dollar peaked at about **¥114.5–114.6** in late August and on 1 September 2026, then fell about **4%** during September. Through October it has stayed between **¥109.4 and ¥110.7**, so it's holding steady rather than still falling.\n\nSo it's a little off its high, but still far stronger than it was in 2023–2025. This data only goes back to 2023, so I can't compare today's rate with earlier years.\n\nIf you're buying yen, for a trip to Japan for example, today's rate is still good compared with recent years, even if it's not quite as good as it was in August.",
